{"causes":["The decline of the Zhou Dynasty led to a fragmented political landscape that necessitated the formal codification of rites and rituals.","The philosophical teachings of Confucius emphasized the importance of social harmony and ethical governance, which influenced the compilation of the Book of Rites.","The Warring States period prompted a reevaluation of traditional practices and the establishment of a unified ethical framework for society."],"effects":["The Book of Rites directly influenced the development of Confucianism as the dominant ideology in Chinese culture and governance.","It established a comprehensive framework for rituals that shaped social norms and political practices for centuries in East Asia.","The text's teachings facilitated the creation of civil service exams, embedding Confucian principles into the bureaucratic systems of later Chinese dynasties."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"confucius","sourceName":"Confucius","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"book-of-rites","targetName":"Book of Rites","context":"Confucius's teachings laid the foundational concepts that were compiled into the Book of Rites during the Warring States period."},{"sourceSlug":"book-of-rites","sourceName":"Book of Rites","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"han-dynasty","targetName":"Han Dynasty","context":"The Book of Rites influenced the Han Dynasty's adoption of Confucianism as the state philosophy, shaping its governance from 206 BCE onward."},{"sourceSlug":"book-of-rites","sourceName":"Book of Rites","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"zhou-dynasty","targetName":"Zhou Dynasty","context":"The text contains essential descriptions of the social structures and rites that characterized the Zhou Dynasty, crucial for understanding its legacy."},{"sourceSlug":"book-of-rites","sourceName":"Book of Rites","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"civil-service-exams","targetName":"Civil Service Exams","context":"The principles outlined in the Book of Rites transformed the structure and content of the civil service examinations instituted during the Sui and Tang dynasties."},{"sourceSlug":"book-of-rites","sourceName":"Book of Rites","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"warring-states-period","targetName":"Warring States Period","context":"The Book of Rites was framed during the Warring States Period as a response to the political fragmentation and ethical crises of the time."}],"places":[{"name":"Luoyang, China","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Qufu, China","role":"Birthplace of Confucius"},{"name":"Beijing, China","role":"Political capital"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Book of Rites","type":"Reference article","slug":"book-of-rites"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q862758","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Rites"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Book of Rites contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Book of Rites (Chinese: 禮記, Lǐjì, 'Records of Ritual') is one of the Five Classics (五經) of the Confucian literary canon — a collection of texts on the norms, administration, and ceremonial usages of the Zhou dynasty, compiled and edited by Han dynasty scholars (particularly Dai Sheng, fl. 1st century BCE) from earlier materials attributed to Confucius's disciples and other Zhou-period sources. Together with the Rites of Zhou (周禮) and the Rites of Etiquette and Ceremony (儀禮), it forms the Three Ritual Texts (三禮) at the heart of the Confucian conception of social and political order. The Book of Rites codifies the ritual forms (lǐ 禮) — the ceremonial practices governing all dimensions of social life from court ceremony and ancestral veneration through marriage, mourning, and hospitality — that Confucianism holds to be the foundation of harmonious social and political order.

The Book of Rites encompasses a remarkably diverse range of material: essays on the philosophy of ritual, descriptions of court and aristocratic ceremony, accounts of the ancient kings' virtuous governance, educational theory, medical knowledge, monthly calendar prescriptions, and two chapters — the 'Great Learning' (大學, Dàxué) and the 'Doctrine of the Mean' (中庸, Zhōngyōng) — that became two of the Four Books of the Neo-Confucian curriculum, the most influential educational canon in Chinese history. The 'Great Learning' articulates the Confucian programme of self-cultivation extending through family, state, and the world ('investigate things → extend knowledge → rectify the mind → cultivate the self → regulate the family → govern the state → bring peace to all under Heaven'), and the 'Doctrine of the Mean' develops the concept of zhongyong (balance, centrality, sincerity) as the foundation of moral cultivation.

As one of the Five Classics, the Book of Rites was central to the Chinese imperial examination curriculum that shaped the education of China's ruling elite from the Han through the Qing dynasties — making its philosophical framework one of the most practically consequential educational texts in world history. Its influence on Chinese social practice (funeral rites, wedding ceremonies, ancestral veneration), court protocol, and educational philosophy extended throughout East Asia.","subjects":["Chinese Philosophy","Confucianism","Classical Era","Chinese History","Ritual","East Asia","Han Dynasty","Imperial Examination"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Book of Rites significantly shaped the governance and social ethics of China, influencing civil service exams and Confucian thought that lasted for over two millennia.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
